# KNOWN GAP (`todo/t0877`), SELF-HOST lane — a closure LITERAL at a
# BUILTIN-METHOD ARGUMENT, where nothing ambient names its return type and
# `guess_return_type` has to answer alone. It has no arm for the body, so the
# closure falls through to `I64_TYPE` and is emitted `int64_t`-returning
# whatever it actually returns:
#
#     error: incompatible types when returning type 'Str' but 'int64_t' was expected
#
# ⚠ THE SUBJECT IS THE POSITION, NOT THE BODY SHAPE — and this fixture is built
# to say so. Its two halves are closure literals in two POSITIONS:
#
#   `result_and_then((String s): Ok(mk(s, "?")))` — a direct free-call
#       ARGUMENT. The callee's declared `Callable[Result[..](..)]` parameter
#       type IS the ambient expected type here (regenerate: `grep -n 'int
#       prev_expected_arg = ctx.expected_type' \
#       tests/fixtures/self_host_lowerer/lower_expr.gg` — the `-1` beside it is
#       an initial clear, immediately overwritten with the peeled param type).
#       `compute_closure_sig` peels its `GtFnPtr` and answers. THIS HALF WORKS.
#
#   `o.unwrap_or_else((String e): e)` — a BUILTIN-METHOD argument. The ambient
#       expected type here is the receiver's PAYLOAD — `enum_category.ok_type`,
#       NOT the wrapper (regenerate: `grep -n \
#       '_combinator_et = _recv_ec_uoe.ok_type' \
#       tests/fixtures/self_host_lowerer/lower_expr.gg`, whose own comment says
#       "uoe returns the PAYLOAD (Some_0 / Ok_0), not the wrapper"). The
#       sibling combinators `or`/`and_then`/`flat_map`/`or_else` DO take a
#       peeled Option/Result wrapper. Here the payload is a `String`, which
#       names no return type, so the peel DECLINES and the body inference is on
#       its own. THIS HALF IS THE GAP.
#
# ⛔ AND THE PEEL DECLINES BECAUSE OF THE TYPE, NOT THE POSITION. Make the
# payload a callable — `Option[Callable[String()]] o`, `o.unwrap_or_else((): h)`
# — and it FIRES, one level too deep, emitting `Str __Closure_0__call` for a
# closure that must return the whole `Callable[String()]`. `todo/t1303`.
#
# ⇒ No widening of a body-shape rule reaches it. It needs the arms themselves:
# Tier-1c closure-param registration, a `lookup_local` beside the `EIdentifier`
# arm's `fn_sigs` read, and element-type propagation through `EMethodCall`.
# Rust gg carries BOTH the ambient override and that fallback and compiles both
# halves; the self-host now carries only the override.
#
# DISCRIMINATE FROM `t0230` (b), which is the same helper's OTHER
# fall-through: a closure whose body's last statement is `SMatch`/`SIf` returns
# UNIT, not I64. Same helper, different arms, different wrong answer.
#
# NOT `t0770`: that class is about reading an ERASED `Callable` PARAMETER's
# declared return type, and its self-host reader
# (`combinator_closure_ret_type`) is fixed. This one is about a closure
# LITERAL's own body, where nothing is erased — the inference has no arm.
#
# INTENDED, on the self-host lane: prints `hello?` then `bad`, rc 0 — matching
# Rust gg, which already does.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

String result_and_then(Callable[Result[String, String](String)] f):
    Result[String, String] o = Ok(mk("hel", "lo"))
    Result[String, String] r = o.and_then(f)
    return r.unwrap()

String identity_closure():
    Result[String, String] o = Error(mk("b", "ad"))
    return o.unwrap_or_else((String e): e)

void main():
    print(result_and_then((String s): Ok(mk(s, "?"))))
    print(identity_closure())
